<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- The light a projector throws, across the corner of the start page that the cards do not use.

     Two straight-edged wedges and nothing else: no curve, no gradient, no group. A television start
     page has a large empty band to the right of three 180dp cards, and this is what stands in it -
     geometry rather than an illustration, because an illustration is the thing that dates.

     The tones are the app's own surface ramp, not an opacity. That is the whole point. A translucent
     layer is a value nobody controls: most sets ship in a Vivid mode whose job is to find
     low-amplitude detail in the dark and pull it up, so "barely visible" is invisible on a calibrated
     panel and a blotch on somebody's television. These are two flat fills the app already draws cards
     and pills with, so the beam is exactly as visible as a card edge - everywhere, by construction -
     it cannot band, and since the ramp is generated at each accent's hue, one file is thirty-one
     pictures rather than one picture ignoring thirty of the themes.

     The geometry is held off the cards on purpose. The outer wedge's leading edge runs from 960,60 to
     520,540, which puts it at x=662 where the card row ends at y=385 - clear of the 620 the cards
     reach. A wedge in colorSurfaceContainer passing behind a card in colorSurfaceContainer would take
     the card's edge with it.

     The viewport is the television's own 960 x 540dp. A set that reports a wider window stretches it
     sideways, which leans the beam a little and does nothing worse; there is no circle in here to
     turn into an ellipse. -->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="960dp"
    android:height="540dp"
    android:viewportWidth="960"
    android:viewportHeight="540">

    <path
        android:fillColor="?attr/accentCard"
        android:pathData="M960,60 L960,250 L720,540 L520,540 Z" />
    <path
        android:fillColor="?attr/accentCardHigh"
        android:pathData="M960,120 L960,235 L790,540 L680,540 Z" />

</vector>
